From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Grische <github@grische.xyz>
Date: Wed, 19 Aug 2026 07:02:19 +0200
Subject: MIPS: dts: intel-mips: cap the FRITZ!Box 7590 at the uncached window

Match the memory node to the top of the uncached window, PHYS_OFFSET +
SZ_256M, the same constant arch/mips/mm/dma-noncoherent.c bounds
uncached aliases with.

Below that line every page has an uncached alias, so a coherent
allocation can be handed one wherever the allocator happens to place it
and the bound holds by construction. Above it there is no alias to give,
and nothing in the page allocator can be asked to prefer one page over
another on that basis -- so as long as the node reaches past the window,
a coherent allocation is a lottery that this board loses once page-cache
churn has moved the free lists up. That was observed: both radios failed
to return from a wifi down/up, and a later boot took a fatal data bus
error on a coherent page at physical 0x31477000, whose alias falls in an
unpopulated part of the register space.

This is a statement about coherent DMA and not about the memory. The
upper 256 MiB is good RAM and the fabric reaches all of it -- cached
access across the full 512 MiB is proven by a 300 MiB cached md5 round
trip, and by a boot that ran its kernel stack at physical 0x309f8000 for
nearly nine seconds without complaint. Only the uncached alias stops
early.

It is the second cap on this node and the first one is instructive. That
one stopped at 0x32310000, a number taken from the CPC base -- the first
populated block in the register space rather than the start of it. It
sat above the true bound, so it cost 221 MiB and still admitted every
address that faults. It has been dropped.

Recovering the full width is parked rather than abandoned. The shape
that would do it is to size ZONE_DMA to the uncached window and have
dma-direct allocate from the zone that has an uncached alias, which
confines coherent allocations without capping anything. Two things keep
it out for now. It
moves ordinary GFP_KERNEL allocations above 0x30000000, which boots into
a reserved instruction exception -- module text is vmalloc-only on this
architecture, so a module function pointer reached through the linear
alias lands on a page that does not hold it; the defect is latent today
and has nothing to do with DMA, and one CONFIG_DEBUG_VIRTUAL boot will
name the caller with %pS rather than leaving an EPC to decode. And it
changes the FRITZ!Box 7560's zone layout as a side effect, turning that
board's entire memory into ZONE_DMA, which is an unvalidated change to
the one board this platform is proven on.

Every reservation in this file and in seale_avm.dtsi is below the cap,
the bootcore window ending exactly at it. The shm RNG seed at 0x3dfb0000
is outside the memory node again, so its reservation returns to being a
supported no-op; the comments on both say so.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-fable-5
Signed-off-by: Grische <github@grische.xyz>

diff --git a/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm_fritz7590.dts b/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm_fritz7590.dts
index 69588093bfe1..71c0845cdc71 100644
--- a/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm_fritz7590.dts
+++ b/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm_fritz7590.dts
@@ -29,52 +29,13 @@ avm-hw-revision {
 	};
 
 	/*
-	 * The board's full 512 MiB, at PHYS_OFFSET 0x20000000.
-	 *
-	 * ## THIS DTB IS SINGLE-KERNEL-ONLY.                      ##
-	 * ## Booting it from a dual-kernel container corrupts or  ##
-	 * ## hangs, and it does so silently at first.             ##
-	 *
-	 * The coupling is exact and it is not a style preference. The
-	 * bootcore's protect_unattached() takes the ram-size = <256> its own
-	 * device tree declares and programs a PM_DDR_GROUP_NONE deny-all
-	 * fabric region over its 32 MiB window at [0x2e000000, 0x2fffffff]
-	 * and over everything above that size, [0x30000000, 0x9fffffff].
-	 *
-	 * The upper half of this board's populated DRAM -- precisely the half
-	 * this memory node now declares -- is then denied to every initiator.
-	 * The failure is a fault or hang on first touch of anything above
-	 * 0x30000000, and it is not the KSEG1-aperture class and not helped
-	 * by staying under that bound.
-	 *
-	 * A single-kernel image starts no bootcore, so the protection pass
-	 * never runs and this node is safe.
-	 *
-	 * Two bounds that did NOT move and still apply at 512 MiB:
-	 *
-	 * The uncached alias of DRAM covers the first 256 MiB only, and stops
-	 * at PHYS_OFFSET + SZ_256M = 0x30000000. The SegCtl maps kernel VA
-	 * 0xA0000000-0xBFFFFFFF uncached onto physical 0x20000000-0x3FFFFFFF,
-	 * and only the lower half of that is DRAM; the upper half is the
-	 * SoC's register space. Cached RAM is fine across the whole node --
-	 * KSEG0 maps 0x80000000 to 0x20000000 and covers all 512 MiB -- but
-	 * anything taking an uncached alias must stay below 0x30000000. hdma,
-	 * which aliases addresses by hand, carries the same bound itself; for
-	 * every other consumer it is the width of this memory node that has
-	 * to keep allocations inside the window, since an ordinary GFP_KERNEL
-	 * page can otherwise come from anywhere in it.
-	 *
-	 * An earlier revision of this comment put the line at PHYS_OFFSET +
-	 * 0x12310000. That address is the CPC -- the first *populated* block
-	 * in the register space rather than the start of it -- and bounding
-	 * at it admitted 35 MiB whose alias faults on the unpopulated holes
-	 * and lands in live registers elsewhere. The CBM pools at
-	 * 0x23800000..0x25200000 that seale_avm.dtsi reserves cannot move,
-	 * only be extended past.
+	 * Capped at the top of the uncached window (PHYS_OFFSET + SZ_256M):
+	 * above it the KSEG1 alias of a page is a register address, so no
+	 * coherent allocation may land there.
 	 */
 	memory@20000000 {
 		device_type = "memory";
-		reg = <0x20000000 0x20000000>;
+		reg = <0x20000000 0x10000000>;
 	};
 
 	aliases {
